WEBVTT
TITLE Variance Of A Sum Covariance And Chebyshev — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.500
Two distributions can share a mean and differ everywhere else.

2
00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:13.000
Variance is the average of the squared distance from the mean.

3
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:19.500
Both bars below have mean 3. One has variance 0, the other 4.

4
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:25.000
The square root is the standard deviation, back in the original units.

5
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:30.000
And a spread of zero means the variable is constant, with probability 1.

6
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:36.000
Four balls numbered 1 to 4. Draw two, without replacement.

7
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:42.000
Twelve ordered pairs, each with probability one twelfth.

8
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:48.000
Each margin is uniform on 1 to 4, so the mean is 2.5 either way.

9
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:54.000
Var of one draw is 1.25: the second moment 7.5 minus 6.25.

10
00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:00.000
One table, two variables, and one number still missing: their drift.

11
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:06.000
Covariance is the average of the product of the two deviations.

12
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:12.000
Cov of X with itself is the variance. That is the same formula.

13
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:18.000
Shortcut: E of XY minus the product of the means. Here 5.833 minus 6.25.

14
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:24.000
Negative: a big first draw leaves a small second one.

15
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:30.000
Divide by the two standard deviations and you get minus one third.

16
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:36.000
Add the two draws and the sum has only five possible values.

17
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:42.000
From that distribution, Var of the sum is 1.667.

18
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:48.000
The three-term identity: 1.25 plus 1.25 plus twice minus 0.4167.

19
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:54.000
So the naive sum 2.5 is wrong here by 0.833, and it is wrong in sign.

20
00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:00.000
The exact count: n sigma squared times (N minus n) over (N minus 1).

21
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:06.000
Now the extreme: let Y be X itself. A fair coin, counted twice.

22
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:12.000
Cov is 1/4, so Var of the sum is 1/4 plus 1/4 plus 1/2, which is 1.

23
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:18.000
The naive answer, 1/2, is half the truth. Same variable, not two.

24
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:24.000
Let Y be 1 minus X and the sum is the constant 1: variance 0.

25
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:30.000
And for a difference the covariance is subtracted, not added.

26
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:36.000
X uniform on minus one, zero, one. Y is X squared.

27
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:42.000
The covariance is zero: the products cancel, three values, signs opposite.

28
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:48.000
Independent would need P of both to equal the product. It is 1/3 against 2/9.

29
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:54.000
So the variance still adds: 2/3 plus 2/9 is 8/9, and it is exact.

30
00:02:54.000 --> 00:03:00.000
Correlation lies in minus one to one, and one means an exact line.

31
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:06.000
For n variables, square the sum and count the cross terms.

32
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:12.000
Three variables: 3 variances and 3 pairs, so 6 terms.

33
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:18.000
Identical and independent: n sigma squared. The mean of n has sigma squared over n.

34
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:24.000
Perfectly correlated: n squared sigma squared. Spread grows like n, not root n.

35
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:30.000
Pairwise zero covariance is enough, even without independence.

36
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:36.000
Markov needs one thing: a non-negative variable with a finite mean.

37
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:42.000
Why: the part above a already carries a times its probability.

38
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:48.000
Here the mean is 1. At a equals 10 the bound 0.1 is attained exactly.

39
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:54.000
At a equals 5 it gives 0.2 where the truth is 0.1. It is a bound, not an estimate.

40
00:03:54.000 --> 00:04:00.000
At a equals 0.5 the bound is 2, which says nothing at all.

41
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:06.000
Chebyshev applies Markov to the squared deviation. The result is 1 over k squared.

42
00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:12.000
k equals 2 gives a quarter, k equals 3 gives 1/9, k equals 4 gives 1/16.

43
00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:18.000
A distribution attains it: mass 1 over 2k squared at plus and minus k.

44
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:24.000
On six-sided die rolls it says at most 0.25 where the truth is 0.

45
00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:30.000
And for k below 1 the bound exceeds 1: it needs finite variance, and k at least 1.

46
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:36.000
Averaging n draws divides the variance by n. That turns into a sample size.

47
00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:42.000
Sigma 2, epsilon 0.5, delta 0.05 needs 320 draws. A normal bound needs 62.

48
00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:48.000
Four steps: identify, check zero covariance, use the identity, keep the units.

49
00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:54.000
Var of 2X is 4 Var X. Var of X plus a constant is Var X.

50
00:04:54.000 --> 00:05:00.000
Every number on this page is computed, and the checks are printed beside it.

